10
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:51,200
Today's guest is Susan Lane, the author of a new book on personal finances that has

11
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,620
already sold half a million copies.

12
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:57,700
Hi Susan, your book is extremely successful.

13
00:00:58,360 --> 00:00:59,680
Why do you think that is?

14
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:05,560
I think that's because of my message, which is making happiness a priority over

15
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:12,200
money. So many of us in my generation have spent decades trying to earn more money

16
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:16,120
just to consume more, but it made us more miserable.

17
00:01:16,120 --> 00:01:21,980
You yourself were once caught in that cycle, working for two decades as an executive

18
00:01:21,980 --> 00:01:26,200
and earning a high salary, but still accumulating debt.

19
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:27,860
I most certainly was.

20
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:29,440
I earned millions.

21
00:01:30,060 --> 00:01:35,840
But by the time I quit my job four years ago to become a writer, I owed over $30

22
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:41,520
,000. So how did you escape that pattern, and what would you advise other people to

23
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:44,640
do? The first change is in what we value.

24
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:50,080
We need to emphasize things that actually make us happy, like relationships, the

25
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:52,600
environment, or even our hobbies.

26
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:58,640
Once we make the right things our priority, our goals will change, and so will our

27
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:03,040
financial behavior. How does that translate into practical action?

28
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:07,680
Can you give our audience examples of what you're talking about on an everyday

29
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:13,940
basis? The major areas for action are usually housing, food, and transportation.

30
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:19,760
So, people might share a with friends instead of living on their own, bring lunch

31
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:25,060
from home instead of going to restaurants, and use public transport instead of

32
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:25,980
owning a car.

33
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:28,420
Those sound like major sacrifices.

34
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:30,700
I could never share my home.

35
00:02:31,140 --> 00:02:32,560
I need my own space.

36
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:34,680
But they aren't sacrifices.

37
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:38,220
When people change their values, their desires change.

38
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:42,060
So in the example of housing, if we value relationships,

39
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:47,780
sharing a home isn't depriving ourselves of space, but giving us an opportunity to

40
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:49,620
spend more time with our loved ones.

41
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:51,840
Indeed it is.

