94
00:07:49,620 --> 00:07:53,000
Forgiveness is the release of resentment or anger.

95
00:07:53,440 --> 00:07:55,400
Forgiveness doesn't mean reconciliation.

96
00:07:56,260 --> 00:08:01,180
We don't have to return to the same relationship, nor do we have to accept the same

97
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:04,040
harmful behaviors from an offender.

98
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:08,560
Forgiveness is vitally important for the mental health of certain victims.

99
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:14,300
It propels people forward rather than keeping them emotionally engaged in an

100
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:16,020
injustice or trauma.

101
00:08:16,940 --> 00:08:22,260
Caring the hurt or anger of an offense leads the body to release stress chemicals.

102
00:08:22,260 --> 00:08:28,040
Eliminating the perpetual flow of those chemicals may also explain why forgiveness

103
00:08:28,040 --> 00:08:30,280
provides physical health benefits.

104
00:08:31,560 --> 00:08:35,200
There are scenarios in which forgiveness is not the best course.

105
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:40,000
Sometimes a victim becomes more empowered when they give themselves permission not

106
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
to forgive. Forgiveness can be challenging.

107
00:08:43,640 --> 00:08:48,260
This is especially true when the offending party offers an insincere apology.

108
00:08:48,260 --> 00:08:52,820
Or maybe they haven't offered anything at all.

109
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:56,280
However, it's often the healthiest path forward.

110
00:08:57,340 --> 00:09:01,320
It's important to cultivate forgiveness by developing compassion for the offender.

111
00:09:02,360 --> 00:09:07,980
Reflect on whether the act was due to malicious intent or whether it was caused by

112
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:11,000
challenging circumstances in the offender's life.

113
00:09:12,120 --> 00:09:13,780
What about forgiving ourselves?

114
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:17,620
We sometimes need to take responsibility for mistakes.

115
00:09:17,620 --> 00:09:23,300
But intense guilt and shame aren't a desirable outcome in the long run.

116
00:09:23,660 --> 00:09:26,500
Forgiving yourself may seem like an ambiguous process.

117
00:09:27,640 --> 00:09:30,820
can begin by acknowledging that you are at fault.

118
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:33,860
Take responsibility for the hurt you caused.

119
00:09:34,820 --> 00:09:37,300
Then reflect on why the event occurred.

120
00:09:38,380 --> 00:09:42,520
Draw the lessons you learned and try to avoid committing a similar offense in the

121
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:49,040
future. Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.

