200
00:18:13,200 --> 00:18:33,800
trail? Recording 2 Why do we form opinions or attitudes about someone or something

201
00:18:33,800 --> 00:18:35,760
without really knowing much about them?

202
00:18:36,840 --> 00:18:42,060
Just hearing something good or bad about a person, a place or thing can influence

203
00:18:42,060 --> 00:18:44,720
our positively or negatively.

204
00:18:45,420 --> 00:18:50,700
But letting the opinions of another person determine what our opinions will be is

205
00:18:50,700 --> 00:18:56,180
dangerous. Forming opinions about someone or something before really knowing them

206
00:18:56,180 --> 00:18:58,360
well is called prejudice.

207
00:18:59,200 --> 00:19:03,380
means before and Judas refers to judgment.

208
00:19:03,760 --> 00:19:08,080
Hence, prejudice means to judge before having adequate knowledge.

209
00:19:09,220 --> 00:19:12,780
We can be prejudiced toward or against someone or something.

210
00:19:13,360 --> 00:19:18,160
In either case, we are only allowing ourselves to see half of the picture.

211
00:19:18,160 --> 00:19:24,340
Very few people or things in this world are all good or all bad.

212
00:19:25,320 --> 00:19:30,080
Prejudiced attitudes are usually based on myths, half -truths or incorrect

213
00:19:30,080 --> 00:19:35,200
information. And they are dangerous because they can keep us from learning the truth

214
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:37,060
about someone or something.

215
00:19:38,340 --> 00:19:42,180
People form prejudices against others for many reasons.

216
00:19:42,860 --> 00:19:47,100
Differences in their race, religion, gender or occupation.

217
00:19:48,620 --> 00:19:50,460
Prejudices keep people apart.

218
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:54,500
They keep us from really knowing and understanding each other.

219
00:19:55,620 --> 00:19:59,340
We should feel proud of who we are and the group of people we represent.

220
00:20:00,580 --> 00:20:05,900
If feelings of pride begin to turn to feelings of superiority when we think that our

221
00:20:05,900 --> 00:20:09,320
group or our beliefs are better than those around us, however,

222
00:20:10,080 --> 00:20:14,400
then we begin to develop prejudiced attitudes that can be harmful.

223
00:20:14,400 --> 00:20:19,700
For example, the prejudiced attitudes of one group may keep another group from

224
00:20:19,700 --> 00:20:24,600
attending certain schools, from living in any neighborhood they want or from getting

225
00:20:24,600 --> 00:20:26,360
a job or a promotion.

226
00:20:27,340 --> 00:20:31,620
Extreme feelings of prejudice have caused the deaths of innocent people.

227
00:20:32,640 --> 00:20:35,700
We are responsible for our own thoughts and opinions.

228
00:20:36,160 --> 00:20:41,020
When we let someone else tell us what to think about someone or something, we are

229
00:20:41,020 --> 00:20:43,460
giving up some control of our own lives.

230
00:20:43,460 --> 00:20:49,220
Before you form an attitude or opinion, find out for yourself about the person or

231
00:20:49,220 --> 00:20:50,560
the thing in question.

232
00:20:51,980 --> 00:20:56,660
Sometimes we don't realize that we hold prejudiced attitudes toward or against

233
00:20:56,660 --> 00:20:57,680
someone or something.

234
00:20:58,500 --> 00:21:04,180
We need to carefully examine our lives and our fears, and to ask ourselves whether

235
00:21:04,180 --> 00:21:09,760
our attitudes come from our personal knowledge and experience or from rumors and

236
00:21:09,760 --> 00:21:10,860
fear of the unknown.

237
00:21:10,860 --> 00:21:15,980
The good news about prejudice is that we are not born with it.

238
00:21:16,600 --> 00:21:19,500
Prejudiced attitudes and opinions develop over time.

239
00:21:20,240 --> 00:21:25,480
But with education and knowledge, we can replace our prejudices with cooperation and

240
00:21:25,480 --> 00:21:50,420
understanding. Question 20.

